/*
RMSTEST_21 - clumping_factor regimes for LANDS
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 3, Sec.6.1's table

QUESTION
The land table has different boundaries from the terrain table. Negative snakey,
0 to 10 irregular, 11 to 40 rounder, and above 40 "lands that extend in one
direction away from the origin". That last regime is the weakest thing in
Sec.6.1. The guide gives one sentence, and the model built on it, a per-land
preferred direction chosen at origin time with weight rising on the projection
onto it, is an invention. It has never been seen.

METHOD
Six lands, one per regime, distinct terrains, identical 600 tile budgets, each
pinned with land_position so they do not compete for space and so the origin is
known exactly rather than inferred. Read circularity for the shape and the
bounding box aspect ratio for the directional claim, because a land that
extends one way is not merely less circular, it is ELONGATED, and those are
different observables. A snake is low circularity with a squarish bounding box.
A directional land is low circularity with a lopsided one.

No borders anywhere, so growth stops only on the tile budget or on another land.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200). Six lands of 600 tiles is 3600 of 40000. Positions
are spread on a 3 by 2 grid at 20, 50 and 80 percent across and 25 and 70
percent down, giving each land about 60 tiles of clearance, which a round 600
tile land does not come close to using.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. Circularity rises across -5, 0, 8, 25 as it should for lands.
2. clumping_factor 60 produces a bounding box whose long side is at least 1.6
   times its short side, while every other land sits near 1.0 to 1.2. This is
   the real test. If the 60 land comes back roughly square, the directional
   regime does not exist and Sec.6.1's 40+ row should collapse into the rounder
   row.
3. If the directional regime IS real, the 40 land and the 60 land both show it,
   which puts the boundary at or below 40 rather than above it.
4. 8, the land default, sits in the irregular band and reads noticeably less
   circular than 25. The terrain table calls 8 rounder. If lands and terrains
   read the same at 8, the two tables are one table and rev 4's split was
   wrong in the other direction.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT
  once per terrain. The bbox column is what settles prediction 2.

WATCH FOR
Each terrain must come back as ONE patch. Two lands growing into each other
merge in the terrain histogram only if they share a terrain, which they do not
here, but a land that hits another land stops early, so check the areas are
near 600 before trusting any shape reading. An area well under 600 means the
land was blocked and its shape records the obstruction.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  /* negative, "extremely snakey, generally not recommended" */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    number_of_tiles 600
    land_position 20 25
    clumping_factor -5
    base_size 2
  }

  /* 0 to 10, "more irregular lands" */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT2
    number_of_tiles 600
    land_position 50 25
    clumping_factor 0
    base_size 2
  }

  /* 8 is the land default (guide:925), the irregular regime */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT3
    number_of_tiles 600
    land_position 80 25
    clumping_factor 8
    base_size 2
  }

  /* 11 to 40, "rounder lands" */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    number_of_tiles 600
    land_position 20 70
    clumping_factor 25
    base_size 2
  }

  /* the 40 boundary itself */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    number_of_tiles 600
    land_position 50 70
    clumping_factor 40
    base_size 2
  }

  /* above 40, "lands that extend in one direction away from the origin".
     The whole reason this map exists. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type ICE
    number_of_tiles 600
    land_position 80 70
    clumping_factor 60
    base_size 2
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
